<BaseLayout title="Welcome to Springfield (the Real One) — Huanyu Chen"
  description="An overnight aurora chase to Oregon in May 2024 that found no aurora, and a Sunday hunting Simpsons murals in Springfield, the town the show's Springfield was named after.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · May 2024</p>
  <h1>Welcome to Springfield (the Real One)</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">We drove all night to chase the northern lights, missed them, and spent the next day hunting Simpsons murals instead.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>One night and one day · May 11–12, 2024</strong><br />
    Los Angeles → Sacramento → the Oregon border, at night → Springfield, Oregon → Eugene airport → Burbank<br />
    zero auroras · most of the length of California driven overnight · about forty murals photographed in a little over two
    hours · one fence with half the town painted on it · no donuts on record
  </div>

  <p>
    Every fan of <em>The Simpsons</em> knows the running joke: nobody can say which state Springfield is in. The show
    has spent more than thirty years dodging the question, putting the town next to mountains, deserts, oceans and
    nuclear plants as each episode requires. But the man who created it has given a straight answer. Matt Groening grew
    up in Portland, Oregon, and he has said that the show's Springfield was named after Springfield, Oregon, a small
    city a hundred miles south of Portland. He picked it, he explained, partly because Springfield is one of the most
    common town names in America, so every viewer could assume it was theirs.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-overview.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-overview.webp'} alt="Map of the day: the University of Oregon in Eugene, the murals of downtown Springfield, a mural fence by the highway, and the Eugene airport." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>The whole day, from the University of Oregon to the Eugene airport.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Groening's Portland is all over the show: Flanders, Lovejoy, Quimby, Kearney
    and Terwilliger are all names of streets in Portland. Homer, Marge, Lisa and Maggie are the names of his own father,
    mother and sisters. So the real Springfield has a fair claim, and in recent years it has leaned into it with
    enthusiasm. Walk around its small downtown today and the yellow faces start appearing on the walls: next to the
    city jail, under a sign for a meat business, around a disabled-parking sign, in an alley full of dumpsters.
  </p>
  <h2>How we ended up in Springfield</h2>
  <p>
    We never meant to go to Oregon. The plan for that weekend was Santa Barbara: an easy drive up the coast, a
    reasonable bedtime. Then, on Friday, May 10th, 2024, the sun did something it had not done in twenty years. A
    run of huge eruptions hit the Earth's magnetic field and set off the strongest geomagnetic storm since 2003, and
    the northern lights were seen far south of anywhere they normally reach. By Saturday the news was full of
    photographs of pink and green skies over Oregon, and there were forecasts of more to come that night.
  </p>
  <p>
    On Saturday morning, friends from Irvine called with a question: did we want to go and chase the aurora? We said
    yes. They drove up to Los Angeles, arriving around noon, and we set off north straight away, all of us in one
    car, up I-5 through the Central Valley, reaching Sacramento by dinnertime. That is where the plans split. Our
    friends had to get their car back to Irvine before Monday, so they could only go as far as the southern border
    of Oregon that night and no further. We wanted to keep going. So at Sacramento we went to the airport, rented a
    car of our own, and both cars drove north into the night toward the Oregon line.
  </p>
  <p>
    The aurora did not come. Whatever the sky did over Oregon that night, we saw none of it. By then, though, we
    were most of the way there, so we kept driving north and reached a hotel near Springfield at about three in the
    morning on Sunday. It was only then that the name sank in. We had long heard that Springfield, Oregon was the
    Springfield, the one the Simpsons are named after, and it seemed impossible that a town like that would not have
    something Simpsons-themed in it. A quick search before sleep turned up the murals. That settled Sunday.
  </p>
  <p>
    What follows is reconstructed from the camera roll, which turns out to be an unusually tidy record of a mural
    hunt: one wall, one photo, one timestamp, and then on to the next wall.
  </p>

  <h2>Eugene, briefly</h2>
  <p>
    Springfield and Eugene are twin cities, separated by the Willamette River and not much else; you can drive from
    one downtown to the other in ten minutes. Eugene is the bigger of the two and home to the <strong>University of
    Oregon</strong>, so the day started there, late in the morning, with a long brunch downtown and a beer at <strong>Max's Tavern</strong>
    on 13th Avenue by campus. Max's is an old, dark college bar, and local lore says it was the model for Moe's
    Tavern, which nobody involved with the show has ever quite confirmed. Then came a short walk on the east side of campus. We
    looked into the lobby of the student recreation center, a tall, bright atrium of glass and pale wood with a
    climbing wall studded with colored holds visible through the windows. In the middle of the floor, guarding the
    entrance, stood a life-size cardboard cutout of the Oregon Duck, the university's famously strange mascot, in
    green and yellow with his orange feet planted wide.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'uo-rec-duck.webp'} alt="The bright, empty lobby of the University of Oregon's recreation center, with a climbing wall in the background and a cardboard cutout of the Oregon Duck mascot." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Oregon Duck, on duty at the rec center on a quiet Sunday.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    It was a Sunday in the middle of the spring term, and the building was almost empty: a few people at the
    far desk, and the Duck. The weather outside was the kind Oregon is not famous for: hot sun,
    a hard blue sky without a single cloud, and new leaves on every tree. It stayed like that all day, which is why
    every photo below has the same sky. We also walked past <strong>Hayward Field</strong>, the university's track
    stadium, with a tall tower clad in perforated metal. Then we crossed the river.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'hayward-field.webp'} alt="The tall perforated-metal tower of Hayward Field beside the stadium's curved roof, under a cloudless blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hayward Field, under that hard blue sky.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>The mural walk</h2>
  <p>
    Downtown Springfield is a grid of a dozen or so blocks along <strong>Main Street</strong>, with low brick and
    concrete-block buildings, wide streets and a lot of parking lots. It is not a place that looks like it is
    expecting tourists, and that is part of the charm. The murals are not on the front of the buildings. They are on
    the backs and the sides, facing alleys and parking lots, next to the electricity meters, the drainpipes and the
    trash bins. You find them by wandering. From the first one, just after one o'clock, to the last in the downtown
    alleys, we spent a little over an hour and a half, mostly on foot.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-murals.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-murals.webp'} alt="Map of the mural walk through downtown Springfield, with round thumbnails of ten murals placed where they were photographed." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>The mural walk: ten of the stops, in the order we found them.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    A word on the artist. Almost every mural on the walk carries the same signature in a corner, sometimes with a
    date: <strong>Bayne Gardner</strong>, or just "Bayne". The dates run from 2019 to 2023, which means this is not
    an old tradition but a recent, steady campaign by one painter, one wall at a time. The style is consistent and
    instantly recognizable: thick black outlines, flat bright colors, characters drawn big and close, usually
    doing something slightly off-script.
  </p>

  <h3>Lisa, a dog and a flying horse</h3>
  <p>
    The first one we found was on a red block wall west of downtown, near the river. Lisa, in her red dress and pearls,
    strides along with her arms out, walking a German shepherd on a leash. The dog is not Santa's Little Helper,
    who is a greyhound. He wears a blue collar with a gold star-shaped tag that reads "Fedo". Beside them, what looks
    like the tail end of a white vintage car sticks out of the wall, fins and all. A little further on, a plain
    <strong>Maggie</strong> in her blue sleeper holds out her arms on a white block wall.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lisa-and-fedo.webp'} alt="A mural of Lisa Simpson in her red dress walking a German shepherd with a star-shaped tag, on a red concrete-block wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lisa and Fedo, the first mural of the day.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'maggie-sleeper.webp'} alt="A mural of baby Maggie in her blue sleeper with her arms out, on a white concrete-block wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Maggie, arms out.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    A few blocks east, by the back of the city's municipal buildings, the murals come thick and fast. On a long beige
    wall, Lisa rides a white winged horse across the bricks, waving, her pony obsession finally rewarded with wings. Around the corner,
    by a fire exit and a dumpster, is a ski scene. On the left, Ned Flanders poses in a skin-tight red and blue ski
    suit, a direct quote of the episode in which Homer, watching him, mutters "stupid sexy Flanders". On the right,
    Homer is bundled up in a puffy jacket and bobble hat, looking cold and doubtful. Between them stands a wooden
    signpost pointing to the runs: The Widowmaker, Spinebuster and Colostomizer.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lisa-pegasus.webp'} alt="A mural of Lisa Simpson riding a white winged horse on a beige brick wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lisa on a winged horse.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ski-flanders.webp'} alt="A mural of Ned Flanders in a tight ski suit and Homer in winter clothes, with a signpost of ski runs between them, above a dumpster." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Ned Flanders, ski suit and all, and a reluctant Homer.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The ski scene sets the tone for the rest of the walk. These are not the family posing for a group portrait. They
    are deep cuts, moments a fan will recognize from one particular episode, painted on a wall where you have to stand
    next to a dumpster to see them.
  </p>

  <h3>Behind City Hall</h3>
  <p>
    A few steps from the city jail, on a grimy white wall by a parked pickup, stands <strong>Chief Wiggum</strong>, in full
    blue uniform with a pink donut in his hand. The placement is either a coincidence or the best joke in town:
    Springfield's most useless police chief, painted a stone's throw from Springfield's real jail, on a donut break.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'wiggum.webp'} alt="A mural of Chief Wiggum holding a donut, on a weathered white wall beside a red pickup truck." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Chief Wiggum, a few steps from the city jail.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Across a parking lot, on a long wall painted a soft sea green, are <strong>Mr. Burns</strong> and
    <strong>Smithers</strong>. Burns is hunched in a green suit, tenting his fingers in the famous "Excellent" pose.
    Smithers stands beside him in a red-and-teal patterned jacket, hands behind his back, a little more relaxed than
    he usually gets to be. The shadow of a tree fell across Burns when we were there, which suited him.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'burns-smithers.webp'} alt="A mural of Mr. Burns tenting his fingers and Smithers standing beside him, on a long sea-green wall, partly in tree shadow." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Mr. Burns and Smithers, half in shadow.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Under an overhang just beyond is the cleverest placement of the day. Homer stands in a shaggy brown <strong>Bigfoot
    suit</strong>, scowling, half hidden behind a real bush that has been allowed to grow up in front of him. It is a
    reference to one of the very first episodes, from 1990, in which Homer gets lost in the woods on a camping trip,
    ends up caked in mud and is mistaken for Bigfoot. Here the bush does the job of the forest. From the sidewalk you
    see a glimpse of fur through the leaves; only when you step under the overhang do you get the full glare.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bigfoot-homer.webp'} alt="A mural of Homer as Bigfoot under a building overhang, partly hidden behind leafy bushes, with the street beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Bigfoot, lurking in the shrubbery.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bigfoot-homer-close.webp'} alt="Close-up of the Bigfoot Homer mural, his scowling face above sunlit leaves." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Up close, he is not pleased to be found.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The wall next to it has a mural with a job to do. A huge fish in pink, blue and green spells out "Keep it Clean" in
    graffiti letters along its body, with "street to stream" underneath. It is a reminder that whatever goes down the
    storm drains here ends up in the river. The fish, naturally, has three eyes. That is a nod to
    <strong>Blinky</strong>, the three-eyed fish caught downstream of Mr. Burns's nuclear plant, which makes it
    probably the only public-service message in America that doubles as a joke about radioactive waste.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'keep-it-clean.webp'} alt="A large mural of a three-eyed fish in pink, blue and green with the words Keep it Clean and street to stream written along its body." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>"Keep it Clean": a three-eyed public-service announcement.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Bright Oak Meats</h3>
  <p>
    On 5th Street, on the side of a small white building under a dark red metal roof, stands <strong>Comic Book
    Guy</strong>. He is sipping a drink through a straw and wearing a T-shirt that reads "Worst. Mural. Ever!", a
    perfect rewrite of his catchphrase about the worst episode ever. A speech bubble beside him says simply "Bayne";
    it's dated 2019, among the earliest on the walk. Someone has added "Hope" and "Love" in purple to the wall beside
    him, along with a small three-eyed orange fish.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'worst-mural-ever.webp'} alt="A mural of Comic Book Guy sipping a drink, wearing a T-shirt that reads Worst Mural Ever, with a small three-eyed fish beside him." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The critics have spoken.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Around the corner the joke gets darker and funnier. "Bright Oak Meats" is painted in big black letters on the
    side of the next building. Right next to the name sits <strong>Lisa</strong>, hugging a little white lamb.
    Lisa famously went vegetarian after meeting a lamb at a petting zoo, so putting her under a sign about meat is
    a choice. Behind her is a campaign poster: "Vote Lisa 2020", a nod to the episode in which she becomes president.
    The mural is dated October 2019, just in time for the campaign. We visited in 2024, by which point the poster
    had become a period piece.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'vote-lisa.webp'} alt="A mural of Lisa Simpson hugging a lamb beside the words Bright Oak Meats, with a Vote Lisa 2020 poster, on a white building with a red roof." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A vegetarian and her lamb, under a sign about meat.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The murals keep going along the same block. Homer holds a pig high over his head in triumph, with a trail of little hoofprints
    running across the wall above them, as if the pig had just walked across the ceiling. It is
    <strong>Spider-Pig</strong>, from <em>The Simpsons Movie</em> of 2007. The pig's body is cleverly painted over a
    boarded-up opening in the wall. A little further on, Marge's sisters <strong>Patty and Selma</strong> stand side by side under
    their purple hair, arms folded, wearing exactly the expression they wear whenever Homer walks into the room. The
    edge of Homer's head pokes into the right of the frame, as if he had wandered over to see what they were
    disapproving of. He shouldn't have.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'homer-spider-pig.webp'} alt="A mural of Homer holding a pig above his head, with hoofprints across the wall, signed Bayne Gardner 2021." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Spider-Pig, held aloft.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'patty-selma.webp'} alt="A mural of Patty and Selma standing side by side with their arms folded, on a white wall next to a utility pole." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Patty and Selma, unimpressed as ever.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    By this point we had worked out the rhythm of the hunt. You round a corner, scan the walls, spot a flash of yellow
    behind a parked truck or a power pole, and go over to stand in front of it. You try to remember the episode, take
    the photo, and look for the next one. The streets were nearly empty on a Sunday afternoon; we had most of the walls
    to ourselves.
  </p>

  <h3>Aliens and three-eyed fish</h3>
  <p>
    The next stretch, off Main Street, was a run of the show's stranger side. Baby <strong>Maggie</strong> sits on a
    gray block wall with a green tentacle curling out from under her: a reference to the Halloween episode in which
    Marge reveals that Maggie's real father is Kang, the drooling one-eyed alien. Around the back of a small building,
    <strong>Mr. Burns</strong> glows a radioactive green in a long gray robe, with sunken eyes and a vague smile.
    That is from the episode in which a "space alien" terrorizes Springfield on Friday nights and turns out to be Burns
    after his weekly treatment to stay alive. The mural is dated 12-30-21.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'maggie-tentacle.webp'} alt="A mural of baby Maggie with a green tentacle curling around her, on a gray concrete-block building." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Maggie, with a tentacle.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'glowing-burns.webp'} alt="A mural of a glowing green Mr. Burns in a gray robe, between two trash bins and a door." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The "alien" of Springfield.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Next to it, on a wall streaked with yellow paint and shadow, another three-eyed fish glowers under the word
    "Wormwood". That is the name of the star in the Book of Revelation that falls from the sky and poisons the
    waters, which is a very literate way to make a joke about Blinky.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'wormwood.webp'} alt="A mural of an orange three-eyed fish with the word Wormwood beside it, on a weathered wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>"Wormwood".</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The glowing Burns stands between two trash bins, in front of a door, under a flagpole. It is a perfectly
    ordinary corner of a small American downtown, which is what makes him so unsettling. The show's
    Springfield has always been an ordinary town where extraordinary nonsense happens on a weekly basis, and this
    corner gets the balance just right.
  </p>

  <h3>The couch</h3>
  <p>
    Then came our favorite. On the dark gray side of a building, under a reserved-parking sign and a "Van
    Accessible" sign, is a lime-green couch, and on it lies <strong>Homer</strong>, in his underpants, asleep, mouth
    open and drooling, a can balanced on his belly. The couch is the most famous piece of furniture on television; the
    show has opened with the family rushing to it for more than thirty years. Here it is painted exactly where the
    van-accessible parking space is, so that the sign reads like a placard for Homer himself. Reserved parking, indeed.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'homer-couch.webp'} alt="A mural of Homer asleep in his underpants on a lime-green couch, painted below reserved-parking and van-accessible signs." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Reserved parking.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    It is signed and dated 2019, one of the oldest on the walk. The paint was still bright, and the sidewalk in front of
    it was empty and sunlit. If there is a single image from the day that
    sums up both the show and the town, it is this one: a man asleep on a couch, painted on a wall facing a parking
    lot, completely at peace.
  </p>
  <p>
    A few minutes later, <strong>Groundskeeper Willie</strong>, enormous and open-mouthed, stared out of a green wall
    signed "© Bayne 2019".
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'willie-donut.webp'} alt="A huge mural of Groundskeeper Willie's head, red hair and beard, mouth wide open, on a green wall signed Bayne 2019." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Groundskeeper Willie, up close.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Springfield in the woods</h3>
  <p>
    On Main Street itself, across the whole side of a tan two-story building, is the biggest and most elaborate mural
    of the day, and it is different from the rest. Instead of one or two characters on a blank wall, it is a full
    scene, painted in the show's own polished style. The family is in an Oregon forest. Tall conifers rise into a blue
    sky, snowy peaks line the horizon, and on a green hill in the middle stands a "Springfield" sign in white letters,
    exactly like the Hollywood sign. Marge paints at an easel on the left. Homer lies in a hammock strung between two
    huge trunks, a can in his hand and a beaver gnawing at the base of one of the trees, which seems unwise. Bart
    crouches on a branch above him with a water balloon, Lisa rides by on a bicycle, Maggie in a helmet rides in a seat
    behind her, and an owl watches the whole thing from a branch.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'springfield-forest.webp'} alt="A large mural of the Simpson family in an Oregon forest, with a Springfield sign on a hill, Homer in a hammock, Marge painting and Lisa on a bike." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Simpsons, at home in Oregon.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    It is the one mural on the walk that feels like an official statement: the show's family placed, at last, in its
    namesake's landscape of firs, rivers and mountains. The shadow of a building cut diagonally across the left side of
    it while we stood there. We took two photos, a few seconds apart, as if the second might somehow
    capture more of it.
  </p>

  <h3>A Treehouse of Horror</h3>
  <p>
    A few blocks north, ten minutes later, on the side of a low cream building on a quiet corner, is a pairing straight out of the
    show's Halloween specials. On the left is a goth in a ragged purple dress, black corset, choker, fishnets and
    heavy boots, with a crest of gray hair. On the right, a green gremlin with yellow eyes and fangs crouches, mid-snarl,
    its claws out. He looks like the gremlin from the "Treehouse of Horror" segment in which Bart spots a monster tearing
    at the school bus. The mural is signed "Bayne 23", among the newest on the walk.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'goth-gremlin.webp'} alt="A mural of a goth character in a purple dress and fishnets beside a green snarling gremlin, on a cream concrete-block building." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A Treehouse of Horror pairing, signed 2023.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Before the alleys we made a loop east, where the murals are farther apart. <strong>Otto</strong>,
    headphones on, kicked his way across one wall beside a white rabbit, and on a black wall nearby a man in a white
    robe held up a dripping chocolate bunny.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'otto.webp'} alt="A mural of Otto with headphones and a backpack, mid-kick, beside red graffiti and a white rabbit sketch on a white block wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Otto, mid-kick.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chocolate-bunny.webp'} alt="A mural of a man in a white robe with his arms out, holding a dripping chocolate bunny, on a black brick wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A chocolate bunny, held aloft.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The family's pink sedan, with a "Student Driver" sign on the door, drove past painted hedges on another wall.
    <strong>Radioactive Man</strong> flexed on the side of a tattoo parlor called Area 51.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pink-sedan.webp'} alt="A mural of the Simpsons' pink sedan with a Student Driver sign, driving past painted hedges and mountains on a white wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The pink sedan, "Student Driver" sign and all.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'radioactive-man.webp'} alt="A mural of Radioactive Man flexing in his red suit and yellow cape on the turquoise wall of the Area 51 Tattoo shop." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Radioactive Man, at Area 51 Tattoo.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The parlor's front wall, naturally, belongs to aliens: a gray one by the door and a tentacled one in a glass dome.
    Nearby, <strong>Lenny and Carl</strong> raised their beers on a green wall.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'area-51-aliens.webp'} alt="The front of the Area 51 Tattoo shop, with a gray alien painted by the door and a tentacled alien in a glass dome on the wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Area 51, with its aliens.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lenny-carl.webp'} alt="A mural of Lenny and Carl raising mugs of beer on a green wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lenny and Carl, cheers.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>The graffiti alley</h3>
  <p>
    The walk ended where it seemed to have been heading all along: in the alleys just south of Main Street, where the
    murals stop being polite. The first thing you see from the street is <strong>Krusty the Clown</strong>, grinning
    across a long wall of red-and-white stripes, with stars for eyes, rainbow hair dripping like melted ice cream and a
    red, white and blue lightning bolt across his face. Two small square windows on either side each frame a grinning
    green elf. It is dated 2019, and it is loud.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'krusty.webp'} alt="A long mural of Krusty the Clown grinning on a background of red and white stripes, with two green elves in square windows, along an empty street." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Krusty, stars and stripes.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Next to Krusty, on a drab beige wall, stands <strong>Moe</strong> in his green apron, arms crossed and scowling.
    He is painted right beside a real window with a small sill, so it looks as if he has stepped out of his bar to
    complain about you. If the lore is right, we had been drinking in the original earlier that day. Around the back of the next building, a startling figure with a bone through her towering blue
    hair, a grass skirt and a necklace of shells is reading a green book called <em>Sometimes a Great Notion</em>, while
    a monkey in a sailor cap rolls past on roller skates. The book is the Springfield detail. Ken Kesey, the author of
    <em>One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest</em>, grew up in Springfield and went to high school here. <em>Sometimes a Great
    Notion</em> is his big novel about a stubborn Oregon logging family. It is a local reference hidden inside a
    cartoon reference, painted above a dumpster.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'moe.webp'} alt="A mural of Moe in a green apron with his arms crossed, beside a real window on a beige wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Moe, stepping out for a scowl.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'great-notion.webp'} alt="A mural of a startled character with a bone in her blue hair reading Sometimes a Great Notion, beside a monkey on roller skates, above a dumpster." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Reading Ken Kesey, a Springfield native.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The alley behind is a continuous wall of graffiti-style murals on a sky-blue background dotted with cartoon clouds,
    the same clouds as the show's opening titles. <strong>Bart and Milhouse</strong> stand back to back, arms folded,
    looking unbothered. A little further along, two Barts are painted against a height chart, one facing forward and
    one in profile: a police mugshot, which, given his record, was only a matter of time. Someone has scribbled red
    graffiti across his chest, which only adds to the effect.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bart-milhouse.webp'} alt="A mural of Bart and Milhouse standing back to back with their arms crossed, against a blue sky with clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Bart and Milhouse.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bart-lineup.webp'} alt="A mural of Bart Simpson in front and side view against a height chart, like a police mugshot." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Bart's mugshot, front and profile.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The biggest pieces are pure graffiti lettering. "HOMER" is spelled out in fat letters coated with pink frosting and
    sprinkles, with his head bursting through the middle, half-asleep and drooling, and flames and smoke at one end.
    A few yards along, "MAGGIE" is written the same way in bubblegum pink, with Maggie herself popping out of the
    middle, arms wide, pacifier in, as the letters sparkle around her. Both sit right behind the alley's dumpsters and
    recycling bins, which turn out to be part of the composition.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'homer-graffiti.webp'} alt="A graffiti mural spelling Homer in pink donut-frosted letters with Homer's drooling face in the middle, behind a dumpster." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>"Homer", in frosting.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'maggie-graffiti.webp'} alt="A graffiti mural spelling Maggie in pink bubble letters with baby Maggie in the middle, against a sky of cartoon clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>"Maggie", in bubblegum.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The best use of a dumpster belongs to <strong>Snake</strong>, the town's resident jailbird. He leans in his leather
    vest at the edge of a painted bonfire, and the flames and black smoke rise directly out of the real blue dumpster
    parked in front of the wall. It is a dumpster fire, in the most literal sense. Across the alley, by an iron gate, a
    gray spiky three-eyed fish grins out of a turquoise square, signed "Bayne 19", the last of the day's many Blinkys.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'snake-dumpster-fire.webp'} alt="A mural of Snake standing by painted flames and smoke that appear to rise out of a real blue dumpster in front of the wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Snake, and a literal dumpster fire.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'spiky-fish.webp'} alt="A mural of a gray spiky three-eyed fish on a turquoise square, beside an iron gate in an alley." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>One more three-eyed fish.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The alley photos are all from a two-minute burst, at 2:41 and 2:42 in the afternoon. You can almost feel the
    pace: nine murals in under two minutes, phone up, trying to get each one before the next caught the eye.
  </p>
  <p>
    On the way out to the fence we passed one more: Bart, flipping the main power switch while Homer lights up in a
    yellow burst.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'bart-main-power.webp'} alt="A mural of Bart flipping a Main Power switch while Homer is electrocuted in a yellow burst, on a blue wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Main power: on.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>The fence</h2>
  <p>
    There was still one more, and it was the best finale the town could have offered. A mile and a half northwest of
    downtown, near the highway, a long wooden fence and the side of a garage have been painted into a single mural
    of the whole town. It runs along a strip of grass, with ordinary houses rising behind it, and the sky painted on
    the fence matches the real sky above almost exactly.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fence-wide.webp'} alt="A long fence and garage painted with a crowd of Simpsons characters, the word Springfield and a huge Homer eating a donut, beside a road and grass." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Springfield fence, end to end.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The left half is a crowd: a double row of Springfield's residents peering over the green hedge as if over a
    garden wall. You can pick out Chief Wiggum with yet another donut, Bart with his slingshot, Carl, someone pointing
    and laughing in true Nelson style, and Blinky again, bright orange this time. "SPRINGFIELD" is written across the hedge in big white letters. On the
    right, the garage wall belongs to a giant <strong>Homer</strong>, taking a bite out of a pink-frosted donut with
    sprinkles, signed "@BayneGardner" in yellow beside him. Across the painted sky behind him flies a creature with a duck's bill and wings of pink frosting and
    sprinkles: part duck, part donut, which in this part of Oregon seems about right.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fence-crowd.webp'} alt="The left half of the fence mural: a crowd of Simpsons characters including Chief Wiggum, peering over a painted hedge with Springfield lettering." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The townspeople, over the hedge.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fence-homer.webp'} alt="A huge mural of Homer eating a pink donut on the side of a blue-painted garage, with Springfield lettering and cartoon clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Homer, and the donut.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Around the corner, on another wall of the same building, Lisa, Marge and Maggie stand in a row against a pink and
    orange sunset sky full of purple clouds, Marge's blue beehive towering over the other two. It is signed and dated
    2021. After two hours of deep cuts, jokes and dumpster fires, it was nice to end with a simple portrait of the
    family's sensible half, standing in the long grass in the afternoon sun.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lisa-marge-maggie.webp'} alt="A mural of Lisa, Marge and Maggie side by side against a sunset sky with purple clouds, above long grass." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lisa, Marge and Maggie, at sunset all day long.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Leaving Springfield</h2>
  <p>
    By a quarter past four we were at the Eugene airport, a short drive west across the valley. We returned the car
    and that evening flew home to Burbank, leaving all those walls behind: Homer on his couch, Burns glowing green, Bigfoot in his
    bush.
  </p>
  <p>
    It was a short trip, and an accidental one. We had set out to see the sky do something it does once in twenty
    years, and we never saw it. What we found instead was a very particular kind of pilgrimage: to a place that
    is famous for being named after a place that doesn't exist. The real Springfield could have ignored the
    connection, or turned it into one statue and a gift shop. Instead it handed its blank back walls to a painter,
    and he filled them with the show's best jokes, one alley at a time.    As Comic Book Guy would say: best. Mural walk. Ever.
  </p>
